evard — actually run with the grid, not against it, reinforcing its regular blocks.","cites":["city-lore","nyc-lpc"],"claim_ids":["c-northern-parallel","c-roosevelt-parallel","c-junction-parallel","c-34ave-parallel","c-37ave-parallel","c-82st-parallel"],"slides":[{"title":"Boulevards That Align","body":"Northern Boulevard, Roosevelt Avenue, and Junction Boulevard all run nearly parallel to the grid, not across it.","map":{"item_id":"865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9","caption":"Major boulevards aligned with the grid axis."},"claim_ids":["c-northern-parallel","c-roosevelt-parallel","c-junction-parallel"]},{"title":"Numbered Streets, Garden Courtyards","body":"34th Avenue, 37th Avenue, and 82nd Street align with the grid, framing blocks built around interior garden courtyards.","map":{"item_id":"81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe","caption":"Grid-aligned streets framing garden apartment blocks."},"claim_ids":["c-34ave-parallel","c-37ave-parallel","c-82st-parallel"]}]},{"title":"Widths That Tell the Story","intro":"The grid's logic shows up in dimension as well as direction: street widths span a huge range, from garden mews to boulevards built for traffic.","cites":["nyc-opendata","nyc-parks"],"claim_ids":["c-mean-width","c-min-width","c-max-width"],"slides":[{"title":"An Average, a Wide Range","body":"Street widths average about 32.6 feet, a figure that masks a dramatic range across the neighbourhood.","map":{"item_id":"81186482d5dd48ca9882593adadd6bfe","caption":"Range of street widths across the neighbourhood."},"claim_ids":["c-mean-width"]},{"title":"From Six Feet to Seventy","body":"Segments range from a 6-foot mews to a 70-foot boulevard — nearly twelve times wider.","map":{"item_id":"865dbb45a3b3455590652560174b9ed9","caption":"Narrowest and widest street segments compared."},"claim_ids":["c-min-width","c-max-width"]}]}]}
